{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Six years you shall sow your field</b> — It is the way of Scripture to speak thus: \"Six days you shall labor and do all your work\" (Exodus 20:9), \"Six days you shall do your deeds and on the seventh day you shall desist\" (Exodus 23:12). And according to the way of our Sages (Jerusalemite Kil'ayim 8:1), \"Six years you shall sow your field\" — and not in the seventh; and this is a negative prohibition that emerges from a positive commandment, which itself constitutes a positive commandment. It turns out that one who sows in the seventh year transgresses both this positive commandment and a negative prohibition. And the midrashic interpretation (Mekhilta Mishpatim 20): Rabbi Yishma'el says — when Israel do the will of the Omnipresent, they observe one shemittah in one week, as it says, \"Six years you shall sow\"; and when they do not do the will of the Omnipresent, they observe four shemittot in one week. How so? They hoe it one year and sow it one year, hoe it one year and sow it one year — it turns out there are four shemittot in one week [of years]."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "I read נָרָהּ as 'they hoe it' (from the root ניר), referring to working the field in alternating years; the midrash counts each year of non-sowing as a shemittah."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:59:26.184941226Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}